1. What is the purpose of the isSafe() function?
Answer:
The isSafe() function checks whether it's safe to place a queen at a given position (row, col) on the board. It ensures that the queen isn't attacked by any other queen already placed on the board. The function checks:
•	The current row to the left (to ensure no other queens are placed there).
•	The upper-left diagonal.
•	The lower-left diagonal.
If no queens are attacking the position, it returns True, otherwise False.
________________________________________
2. What is the role of the solveNQUtil() function?
Answer:
The solveNQUtil() function is the recursive backtracking function that tries to place queens on the board column by column. It attempts to place a queen in each row of the current column and recursively tries to place queens in the next column. If it finds a valid configuration (i.e., all queens placed without conflicts), it returns True. If not, it removes the queen (backtracks) and tries another row. This continues until all queens are successfully placed or all possibilities are exhausted.
________________________________________
3. How does the backtracking work in this code?
Answer:
Backtracking is used when the algorithm places a queen in a position but later discovers that the configuration doesn't lead to a solution. In such cases, the algorithm removes the queen (backtracks) and tries a different position. Specifically, in the solveNQUtil() function, if placing a queen in a particular row doesn't lead to a valid solution, the queen is removed (board[i][col] = 0), and the algorithm tries placing the queen in the next row of the current column. This process continues until all queens are placed successfully or no valid solution is found.
